Scenario
Your team has recently deployed a Code Assistant Agent built on the Agent Development Kit (ADK). When developers submit queries, a Coordinator orchestrates two specialized sub-agents: a DocsAgent (querying official documentation on managed Agent Search) and a CommunityAgent (querying past Stack Overflow discussions).

While the assistant is highly accurate, operational costs are too high. A single query fans out into multiple coordinator turns, sub-agent invocations, document retrievals, and expensive reasoning tokens.

As the Lead Generative AI Platform Engineer, you must audit the baseline query cost, apply a series of cost-reduction levers, and establish an optimized model configuration that drastically slashes the bill while maintaining the required answer quality floor.

Your challenge
Your final challenge is to configure the Code Assistant Agent's control knobs (MY_MODELS, MY_THINKING, and MY_TOPK) to successfully analyze a held-out dataset of 16 complex developer queries under the following production constraints:

Cost budget: Average cost per query must be at or below $0.0135 USD (roughly half of the baseline cost).
Quality floor: Average quality score must be at or above 0.90 (evaluated by a state-of-the-art LLM judge model).
Architectural constraints:
Coordinator tier: The Coordinator seat cannot use the budget gemini-3.1-flash-lite tier. Experiments show that this tier is too weak to handle complex orchestration, leading to broken formatting and bad synthesis. It must run on either Pro (gemini-3.1-pro-preview) or Flash (gemini-3.5-flash). Sub-agents are unrestricted.
Embedding model: Must remain pinned to the appropriate embedding model (gemini-embedding-001).
Completion verification: You must successfully run the final challenge evaluation cells, achieve a green PASS verdict, and ensure the final_metrics.json file is successfully compiled and uploaded to your designated Google Cloud Storage results bucket.
Task 1. Open the JupyterLab on Agent Platform Workbench
In this task, you open the managed JupyterLab environment where you run the lab notebook. The lab provisions an Agent Platform Workbench instance for you when it starts.

In the Google Cloud Console, on the Navigation menu (Navigation menu), click Agent Platform > Notebooks > Workbench. Alternatively, you can type Agent Platform Workbench in the top search bar and click the first result.

Find the generative-ai-jupyterlab instance and click Open JupyterLab.

The JupyterLab interface for your Workbench instance opens in a new browser tab.

Task 2. Get the lab files
In this task, you confirm that the lab notebook and its supporting files are available in your JupyterLab home directory. The lab copies them there for you when the instance starts.

Confirm the lab files are present
In the JupyterLab File Browser on the left, look for the following files in your home directory:

the code_assistant_cost_optimization.ipynb notebook
the code_assistant folder (the plumbing package that the notebook imports)
the assets folder (the retrieval corpora and evaluation datasets)
If the files are present, continue to Task 3.

Copy the lab files manually (only if they are missing)
If the files are not in your home directory, copy them from the lab Cloud Storage bucket.

In JupyterLab, click File > New > Terminal to open a terminal.

To copy all of the lab files into your home directory, run the following command:

gcloud storage cp -r "gs://qwiklabs-gcp-04-defacf1d0ff9-bucket/*" ~/
Copied!
In the File Browser, confirm that the notebook and the code_assistant and assets folders now appear in your home directory.

Task 3. Run the lab notebook
In this task, you complete the hands-on work inside the notebook. The notebook contains its own step-by-step instructions, code, and explanations. Run the cells in order from top to bottom, and let each cell finish before you start the next. The measured runs are the slow cells; the notebook states the expected duration next to each.

Note: Several cells call billable services: Gemini models (including reasoning tokens), the Gen AI evaluation service's judge model, Agent Search queries, and embeddings. Some results are designed to surprise you: one model-tiering step intentionally shows high cost variability, and the final challenge stops with an AssertionError until you fill in your own configuration. That is the exercise, not a defect; the notebook explains the result to expect at each step.
In the File Browser, double-click code_assistant_cost_optimization.ipynb to open it.
If you are prompted to select a kernel, select the default Python 3 kernel.
Run the install cell, then run the kernel-restart cell and wait for the kernel to come back.
Follow the instructions in the notebook to measure the agent's baseline cost, apply the three optimization levers, and complete the graded final challenge, which is required to complete the lab.
Task 4. Provision the Agent Search resources
In this task, you provision the managed Agent Search (Agent Search on Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform) data store and search engine. The lab notebook automates this provisioning process programmatically using the Google Cloud SDK inside the JupyterLab environment.

Run the notebook cells under Part 0: Setup, the cost model, and the ledger and its subtask, Provision retrieval and assemble the agent.
The code_assistant-docs data store and code_assistant-docs-engine search engine are provisioned using the following docs_sc variable:
DOCS_SC = vais.ensure(PROJECT, VAIS_LOCATION, DS_ID, ENGINE_ID, load_jsonl("assets/docs.jsonl"))
Copied!
This function connects to the Discovery Engine API to provision the following:

Data Store ID: code_assistant-docs
Search Engine ID: code_assistant-docs-engine
